From b6c0eaeab1617920c42727a605ae786bfbb0385f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Fri, 21 Aug 2026 17:04:36 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] layout: flatten computed styles into rasterized inline SVG
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Inline <svg> is XML-serialized and rasterized by resvg with no CSS
context, so selector-driven styling on the subtree — class rules for
fill, stroke, stroke-width and friends — was lost entirely: icon sets
styled via stylesheet classes rendered with initial paints.

Serialization (SVGSVGElement::serialize_and_cache_subtree) now stamps
each element in the cloned subtree with a data-servo-style-id attribute
recording its preorder position; the ids depend only on structure, so
the cached serialization stays valid across restyles. At layout time,
the replaced-content path walks the original subtree's computed styles
in the same preorder and injects a <style> block of
[data-servo-style-id="n"] rules (usvg parses <style> via simplecss,
including attribute selectors and !important) carrying each element's
computed SVG paint properties. Declarations are !important so they
outrank presentation attributes and inline style in the serialized
markup — computed values already reflect the full cascade. currentcolor
is resolved at flatten time against each element's computed color, since
svgtypes only parses the camelCase currentColor spelling. As with the
root color attribute, the rewritten data: URL doubles as the cache key,
so identical style states rasterize once and restyles re-rasterize.

Because SVG descendants generate no boxes, a restyle inside the subtree
(for example a theme class flip recoloring icons) previously surfaced
only as repaint-level damage and left a stale rasterization on screen;
the damage traversal now escalates any damage arriving at an <svg>
element to a box rebuild so rasterization inputs are recomputed.

Known limitations: display:none from CSS on descendants is not honored
(Servo computes display:none for the whole boxless subtree, so the
computed value carries no author signal; visibility:hidden works), and
subtrees cloned by <use> expansion keep the referenced element's id, so
they flatten with the referenced element's at-definition styles.
